<section class="section"><div class="wrap content">
<h2>What is IRR in real estate?</h2>
<p>The internal rate of return is the yearly rate that ties a whole deal together: the cash you put in at purchase, the cash flow you collect each year, and the money you take out at the sale. Unlike a simple return, IRR respects <em>when</em> money moves — a dollar of profit in year 2 is worth more than the same dollar in year 10, and IRR is the rate that makes all of those timed amounts balance exactly.</p>
<div class="timeline-box"><p class="timeline-title">The cash-flow timeline</p><div class="timeline-steps"><div class="timeline-step"><span class="step-badge">0</span><div class="step-body"><p class="step-when">Year 0 — the money in</p><p class="step-formula">−(down payment + closing costs + initial repairs)</p></div></div><div class="timeline-step"><span class="step-badge">1–n</span><div class="step-body"><p class="step-when">Each year — the running cash flow</p><p class="step-formula">rent after vacancy − operating expenses − loan payments − capital expenses</p></div></div><div class="timeline-step"><span class="step-badge step-badge-final">n</span><div class="step-body"><p class="step-when">Final year — the sale</p><p class="step-formula">that year’s cash flow + (sale price − selling costs − remaining loan balance)</p></div></div></div><p class="timeline-result">IRR is the rate <em>r</em> that makes the present value of every line above add up to zero.</p></div>
<h2>Worked example</h2>
<p>Buy at $350,000 with 25% down and 2% closing costs — $94,500 in at year 0. Rent starts at $39,600 a year and grows 3% annually; expenses grow 2.5%. After ten years the property is worth $493,710 at 3.5% yearly appreciation; selling costs and the remaining $222,537 loan balance leave $241,550 from the sale. Add ten years of cash flow ($80,717) and the deal returns $227,767 of profit — an IRR of <strong>15.09%</strong>, or 3.41× the cash invested.</p>
<h2>What is a good IRR for a rental property?</h2>
<p>Under 8% is modest for a leveraged rental — a broad index fund has historically competed with that, with far less work. Between 8% and 12% is fair and typical of stable, cash-flowing properties. From 12% to 18% is solid — the range most experienced investors target. Above 18% is excellent, and usually comes with value-add work, a below-market purchase, or extra risk that deserves a second look.</p>
<h2>IRR vs ROI vs cash-on-cash</h2>
<p>Cash-on-cash looks at one year only: the first year’s cash flow over the cash invested (4.34% here). ROI totals the whole deal’s profit but treats a ten-year hold and a three-year hold alike. IRR is the only one of the three that prices time itself — which is why the same property can show a modest cash-on-cash, a large total ROI, and a mid-teens IRR all at once.</p>
<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul><li>IRR turns a whole hold — purchase, yearly cash flow and sale — into one annualized rate.</li><li>It rewards money that arrives early, so faster rent growth lifts IRR more than late appreciation.</li><li>Most of a typical deal’s profit still comes from the sale; here, 65% of it.</li><li>IRR is only as honest as its growth assumptions — test a weaker appreciation rate before trusting it.</li></ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details class="faq"><summary>What does IRR mean in simple terms?</summary><p>It is the yearly return your money effectively earns inside the deal, accounting for the fact that profit arrives at different times. If the IRR is 15%, your invested cash grew as if it earned 15% a year, every year, for the whole hold.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>Why is my cash flow negative but the IRR positive?</summary><p>That happens when appreciation and loan paydown do the heavy lifting: you feed the property a little each year, then the sale repays you with interest. It can work, but it is speculation on prices rather than income — the risk is higher.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>What IRR do investors aim for?</summary><p>Most target 12–18% on leveraged rentals. Below 8%, many decide the effort and risk are not paid for; above 18%, they check the assumptions twice.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>Does IRR include the sale of the property?</summary><p>Yes. The final year adds the net sale proceeds — sale price minus selling costs and the remaining loan balance — which is usually the largest single cash flow in the timeline.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>IRR or cap rate — which should I use?</summary><p>Cap rate screens a property in one year, ignoring financing. IRR judges a full deal over time, including the loan and the exit. Screen with cap rate; decide with IRR.</p></details>
